{"kind":"report","text":"Independent exact-head review of join and redemption hardening 812fb9076ba56441b14efcc19115cb720b18ae94: changes requested for one bounded client recovery defect. All 11 artifacts, the implementation request, the contract edits and their changed paths were inspected. The candidate is based on a6330262; main has since incorporated plan003 independently. No canonical source was changed.\n\nP2 — recovery ignores the entry point's supplied clock (packages/client/src/connect.ts:152 and :165; ClientOptions.now is declared in packages/client/src/wire.ts:45). join accepts ClientOptions, but the new recoverJoin session-request path calls signRequest without its clock argument, so Date.now is used even when the caller supplied options.now. Ordinary client request signing uses the supplied clock. A fresh join succeeds with a virtual Room clock, but after losing that reply the recovery request is refused because notAfter is outside the Room's 300-second window. This is a test-clock/API consistency defect; default-clock production recovery and the three security repairs pass.\n\nExact-source reproduction: checker-join-recovery.test.ts in /tmp/artroom-checker-sec-join-v1-diagnostics/packages/room/test/workerd has the named test 'checker: join recovery honors the supplied client clock for its signed session request'. It sets the actual Room's clock one day before system time, gives join now: () =\u003e clock.now, and drops the first HTTPS redemption reply after the Room admitted the join. Recovery fails with the precise notAfter refusal. Passing only options.now (defaulting to Date.now) into recoverJoin and using it as signRequest's fourth argument makes all three checker integration tests pass; restoring exact production source restores the one failure. Logs: /tmp/artroom-checker-sec-join-v1-recovery-clock-option.log, -recovery-clock-control.log and -recovery-clock-restored.log. The control changed only scratch source, which was restored.\n\nRequired repair: pass the configured clock through the recovery helper, evaluate it for every newly signed session retry, and add a named virtual-clock lost-reply recovery control. Preserve the original join bytes/idempotency key, proof of possession via signed session requests, and the replay refusal. Retain all three security repairs and the full scope.\n\nArchitecture: the admission layer now returns replay provenance explicitly, with submit preserving its previous result API. The requests layer uses that provenance to issue a session only for a join admitted by this call. Refused joins return before commit on every admission path, preserving ordinary refused-act recording. docs/protocol.md reconciles R-ADM-8, R-GEN-6 and R-SEC-4, updates R-CRED-9, and maps the adapter changes in section 31. The nullable internal address distinguishes HTTPS callers from service bindings without changing RoomWire.redeem's contract. The new recovery path needs the existing clock option carried through.\n\nSecurity: the requested repairs are verified. A copied public signed join cannot mint a session through HTTPS, direct DO or RPC replay, including simultaneous identical redemptions. A policy-refused join seals neither secret nor idempotency result and leaves the invitation unused; other refusals still record. All join paths share an issued-invitation limit, both custody branches are counted, unrelated/invalid invitation input opens no invitation counter, HTTPS addresses retain their separate limit, and RPC callers do not share a fabricated address. Per-Room WeakMap windows expire and remain capped at 10,000, with fail-closed capacity behavior; counters use no durable row writes. Reset on eviction is explicit and allowed by the updated contract. Recovery retrieves the public record but obtains a session only by signing with the joining key. Provider calls used only local fakes; no deploy or live credential was used. Other hygiene work remains tracked on its separate review lane.\n\nSimplification: the explicit replay result and shared limiter remove the path-dependent recordRefusals hook and process-global unbounded map. The bounded per-Room window scan is proportional to expired entries removed, not a scan of every live counter. Client recovery is small and retains the existing transports and retry mechanism. Carrying the existing clock parameter is sufficient for the remaining repair; no new protocol, public type or workflow is needed.\n\nIndependent validation: fresh npm ci, root typecheck and root npm test exit 0 on the exact clean candidate: 1,991 passed and one skipped; Git Node 221, Room Node 125, Room workerd 407. The targeted Room integration file passes 13/13 and client redemption file 15/15 in a separate diagnostics clone. Independently broke all 13 reported behavior guards; each causes named runtime failures without compilation/module errors. Removing only the invitation string-type check stays green for the malformed-input controls, consistent with the documented equivalent case; it is not counted as a killed mutant. Exact source was restored and both targeted sets pass again. Additional actual Room controls pass for HTTPS lost-reply recovery and RPC recovery after a real Room eviction, each with a working recovered read session and one join. The supplied-clock diagnostic is the sole remaining checker failure; the narrow clock-positive control passes and restored exact source fails.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:496965889119a0a621ba2787ecce83149b3bac74","binding":"assigned","head":"812fb9076ba56441b14efcc19115cb720b18ae94","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:20c674e5887291dec46930f7009078b0c76143ae\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:86123511453e26cb3a649a617af5e1051c968591\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a04baf61e825ebaad5e74f4661044c9a83190be9